  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 247 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThe progressive, high amplitude pattern continues into next week as \nanother trough moves across the Rockies Sunday night and into the \nGreat Plains Monday.  Lee-side cyclogenesis forced by the trough \nproduces a strong low level low over the Great Plains Monday, and \nthe system moves slowly into the Midwest by Tuesday morning. \nDeterministic guidance shows some differences in the speed and \namplitude of the trough as it drifts northeast through midweek, but \nthose differences do not look to affect the sensible weather at the \nsurface across Missouri and Illinois too much.  Showers and a few \nthunderstorms develop ahead of the cold front associated with this \nsystem on Monday over the eastern Plains and move east along with \nthe cold front as it drifts through the Midwest.  Current thinking \nis that rain will hold off in our area until Monday night, and then \nwill move slowly east across our area from after 06Z Tuesday through \nearly Wednesday morning.  While deep-layer shear ahead of the front \nis forecast to be in the 45-60kt range, persistent clouds and \nshowers will put the damper on heating and instability.  While there \nwill likely be occasional rumbles of thunder Monday night through \nTuesday night the lack of instability should minimize the severe \nthreat.  Additionally, a persistent blocking surface ridge along the \nGulf Coast will keep the richest Gulf moisture from moving up into \nthe Mid Mississippi Valley ahead of the cold front.  While P-wats \nare relatively high for early November in the 1.5 to 1.8 range ahead \nof the front, the lack of instability should also reduce the threat \nfor heavy rainfall as it does with severe storms.  This period of \nrainfall through Tuesday night/early Wednesday should therefore be \nbeneficial with little if any threat for flooding.\n\nThe details in the model guidance diverge further after the front \nfinally moves through on Wednesday, but the generally unsettled \npattern looks to continue.  Another wave moves across the Plains and \nMidwest later in the week, though there may not be much moisture in \nthe wake of Wednesday's FROPA for this next system to tap into.  The \nshort wave does look like it will amplify the long wave pattern \nover the eastern 1/2 to 2/3s of the United States for Friday and \nSaturday.  This allows cooler air to filter into the CONUS east of \nthe Rockies bringing highs down from the low and mid 70s during the \nfirst 1/2 of the week to the mid 60s for the latter half.  In fact, \nNBM max temps are likely being affected by warm bias-corrected \nguidance it incorporates, so highs later in the week may end up \ncooler than currently forecast.",
